<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guadalajara (Spain)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guadalajara is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, capital of the province of the same name in Castilla–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, on the Henares river north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. Its name comes from the Arabic for 'valley of stones', from its time as a Muslim frontier town before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a> took it around 1085. The province covers part of the ancient land of the Celtiberians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadalajara-spain.jpg" alt="Guadalajara (Spain)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Malopez 21 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guadalajara,_vista_a%C3%A9rea_(tone,_contrast,_color).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guadalajara lies in the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> at about 685 metres above sea level, on the left bank of the Henares river, between the river and the moors of La Alcarria. The historic core stands on a small rise between two small watercourses, which gave an easily defended site. Control of the scarce water supply was fought over by the local nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Roman mansio called Arriaca is presumed to have stood in the region, though there is no archaeological proof. The town was founded in the Islamic period, in the 9th century, and was officially known as Madīnat al-Faraŷ, after a person named Faraŷ. It was later known as Wādī al-Ḥijāra, from which the present name comes. It belonged to the Middle March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, governed by the Masmuda Berber clan of the Banū Sālim for the Umayyad rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. A fortress, walls and the bridge over the Henares date from the Muslim period.</p><p>Alfonso VI of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> annexed it in 1085 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, and the town surrendered without resistance, although tradition says a force led by Álvar Fáñez de Minaya seized it by night. Alfonso VII gave the first fuero in 1133. Henry IV granted city status in 1460. The Mendoza family, Dukes of El Infantado from 1475, dominated the city for generations and patronised the arts, so that it was called the Atenas alcarreña, Alcarria's Athens. A large Jewish community maintained four synagogues until the expulsion of 1492.</p><p>The 17th century was a time of crisis, made worse by the expulsion of the moriscos in 1610, which took 10 percent of the population, and the Mendozas left for good in 1657. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> the city was sacked and its population fell to about 2,200. The French took Guadalajara in 1808 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>, and it became a provincial capital in 1833.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Guadalajara is the capital of its province in Castilla–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, with 93,470 registered inhabitants, and has grown quickly since 1959, when it was included in plans to relieve the congestion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>'s industrial estates. The Palacio del Infantado, the bridge over the Henares, built on Roman foundations, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> co-cathedral of Saint Mary are among its monuments. In March 1937, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, Republican forces defeated an Italian expeditionary force near the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guadalajara, Spain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lusones lived on the high plateau around the sources of the Tagus and the Jalón, between modern <b>Guadalajara</b> and Zaragoza.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusones">Lusones</a></li></ul></section>
